Analysis and Discussion

To better understand how the models behaved in the few-shot setting, we performed a label-level analysis on the K=5 experiments. The goal was to compare how the single BART teacher model and the distilled BERT student predicted different entity types, and to see whether there were any unusual patterns in the data that could affect the final results.

Overall, the distilled BERT student performed noticeably better than the vanilla BERT baseline and also achieved competitive results compared to the original OADA setup. The improvements mainly came from better predictions on PER and LOC entities. These two categories showed the strongest gains after distillation, suggesting that the teacher ensemble successfully transferred useful information to the student model.

However, not all entity types improved equally. ORG remained one of the most difficult categories for all models. In several cases, organization names were either missed completely or confused with locations. This was especially common for sports teams and institutions, where the surface form of the entity often resembles a location name. Interestingly, the single BART teacher sometimes performed slightly better than the distilled BERT student on ORG entities, which may suggest that some information is lost during the distillation process.

We also observed that the distilled BERT student behaved more conservatively than the teacher models. The student achieved relatively high precision but lower recall, meaning that when the model predicted an entity it was often correct, but it also failed to detect many gold entities. This behavior is likely caused by the extremely low-resource K=5 setting, where the model has very limited labeled examples during training.

Another observation was that MISC entities remained difficult for all models. The scores for this category were consistently low, most likely because MISC is a broad and heterogeneous category with fewer consistent patterns compared to PER or LOC.

Finally, we performed a small sanity check on the evaluation setup to investigate whether sequence truncation affected the reported results. Only two test examples were truncated under the max_length=128 evaluation setting, and both examples consisted mainly of long sports-related entity lists. Because so few examples were affected, truncation does not appear to have a significant impact on the final performance.

Overall, the analysis suggests that the teacher-student distillation setup successfully improves few-shot NER performance while still allowing efficient BERT inference at test time. At the same time, the experiments highlight that certain entity types, especially ORG and MISC, remain challenging in extremely low-resource settings.
